WEBVTT

00:00:01.800 --> 00:00:06.749
The lab draws a picture of 256 by 256 places.

00:00:07.100 --> 00:00:10.343
Change it to 512 by 512.

00:00:10.690 --> 00:00:16.770
Does the place i = 64, j = 64 still turn into the same pair of fractions?

00:00:17.120 --> 00:00:18.059
Yes or no.

00:00:18.410 --> 00:00:19.541
Pause and write it.

00:00:27.690 --> 00:00:34.232
Lab 1 of MIT course 6.S894, Accelerated Computing, asks for the same picture to be

00:00:34.232 --> 00:00:40.934
computed four ways: on a CPU core, on a CPU core with 8-at-once instructions, on the

00:00:40.934 --> 00:00:45.482
GPU with a single thread, and on the GPU with 32 threads.

00:00:45.830 --> 00:00:47.899
The picture is the Mandelbrot set.

00:00:48.250 --> 00:00:54.039
For now, all that matters is this: the picture is a sheet of 256 by 256 whole

00:00:54.039 --> 00:01:00.280
numbers, each the result of a small amount of arithmetic, stored in an array called

00:01:00.280 --> 00:01:00.581
out.

00:01:00.930 --> 00:01:05.410
I got stuck before any GPU appeared, on the first two lines of arithmetic.

00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:09.899
I believed the place (i, j) in the picture is the thing being computed.

00:01:10.250 --> 00:01:11.231
It is not.

00:01:11.580 --> 00:01:16.699
A pair of code lines turns (i, j) into a different pair of fractions, which I named

00:01:16.699 --> 00:01:21.201
the middleman pair, and only the middleman pair goes into the arithmetic.

00:01:21.550 --> 00:01:26.955
Hold this question while you calculate: is (9, 7) the same point on every picture

00:01:26.955 --> 00:01:27.289
size?

00:01:34.440 --> 00:01:40.406
In English: i takes 0 up to img_size minus 1, and inside it j does the same, so

00:01:40.406 --> 00:01:42.973
every pair (i, j) is visited once.

00:01:43.330 --> 00:01:51.282
Turn j and img_size into fractions, divide, multiply by 2.5 and subtract 2.0; that

00:01:51.282 --> 00:01:51.863
is cx.

00:01:52.210 --> 00:01:54.407
The f marks a 32-bit fraction.

00:01:54.760 --> 00:01:58.984
Do the same with i, but subtract 1.25; that is cy.

00:01:59.330 --> 00:02:05.486
A loop, not shown here, turns cx and cy into a whole number, iters, stored at place

00:02:05.486 --> 00:02:07.266
i times img_size plus j.

00:02:07.620 --> 00:02:20.343
My program had img_size = 256 and max_iters = 1000, so out holds 256 × 256 = 65,536

00:02:20.343 --> 00:02:22.489
whole numbers.

00:02:22.840 --> 00:02:27.853
Notice that the cx line reads only j, and the cy line reads only i.

00:02:28.200 --> 00:02:33.234
Draw out as a sheet, with i counting down from the top and j counting right from the

00:02:33.234 --> 00:02:33.533
left.

00:02:33.880 --> 00:02:35.395
That is the image paper.

00:02:35.750 --> 00:02:40.635
Draw every middleman pair as a point on a second sheet with (0, 0) at its center.

00:02:40.980 --> 00:02:42.431
That is the graph paper.

00:02:42.780 --> 00:02:47.025
The loop reads only cx and cy; it never sees i or j.

00:02:47.380 --> 00:02:48.148
Pause.

00:02:48.500 --> 00:02:49.375
Long-hand.

00:02:55.720 --> 00:03:06.515
Here 64/256 = 0.25, and 0.25 × 2.5 = 0.625.

00:03:06.870 --> 00:03:19.777
Subtracting 2.0 gives cx = −1.375; subtracting 1.25 instead gives cy = −0.625.

00:03:20.120 --> 00:03:29.574
So the place (64, 64) on the image paper becomes the point (−1.375, −0.625) on the

00:03:29.574 --> 00:03:30.957
graph paper.

00:03:31.310 --> 00:03:32.078
Pause.

00:03:32.430 --> 00:03:33.539
Fill the edges.

00:03:39.890 --> 00:03:50.403
For j = 0, cx = −2.0, and for i = 0, cy = −1.25; that is where the graph paper

00:03:50.403 --> 00:03:51.346
begins.

00:03:51.690 --> 00:04:07.391
For j = 255: 0.99609375, then 2.490234375, then cx = 0.490234375.

00:04:07.750 --> 00:04:11.355
For i = 128, cy = 0.

00:04:11.700 --> 00:04:17.567
The step between neighbouring j is 0.009765625.

00:04:17.920 --> 00:04:28.437
Because j/256 never reaches 1, cx never reaches −2.0 + 2.5 = 0.5.

00:04:28.780 --> 00:04:35.500
The number 2.5 is 0.5 − (−2.0), the distance the code covers.

00:04:35.850 --> 00:04:42.728
The lab author typed 2.5, 2.0 and 1.25 into the code; the mathematics does not

00:04:42.728 --> 00:04:43.786
supply them.

00:04:44.140 --> 00:04:44.908
Pause.

00:04:51.260 --> 00:05:00.711
At img_size 32 the pair is (−1.453125, −0.546875).

00:05:01.060 --> 00:05:11.940
At 256 it is (−1.931640625, −1.162109375).

00:05:12.290 --> 00:05:17.207
It is the same (9, 7) on the image paper, but two different points on the graph

00:05:17.207 --> 00:05:17.581
paper.

00:05:19.730 --> 00:05:20.498
Pause.

00:05:20.850 --> 00:05:21.874
Fill all of it.

00:05:22.220 --> 00:05:27.045
Then write one sentence that is true of everything on this page: img_size changes

00:05:27.045 --> 00:05:29.665
something, and never changes something else.

00:05:36.020 --> 00:05:45.428
The step is 0.0048828125, and cx at j = 0 is still −2.0.

00:05:45.780 --> 00:05:58.596
The largest j is 511, its cx is 0.4951171875, and the gap to 0.5 is 0.0048828125,

00:05:58.596 --> 00:06:00.969
half of before.

00:06:01.310 --> 00:06:16.478
The pair (−1.375, −0.625) moved to (128, 128), and (64, 64) now gives (−1.6875,

00:06:16.478 --> 00:06:18.206
−0.9375).

00:06:18.560 --> 00:06:23.554
So img_size changes how many middleman pairs are taken from the graph paper, which

00:06:23.554 --> 00:06:25.259
is how fine the sampling is.

00:06:25.610 --> 00:06:33.499
It never changes which part of the graph paper is covered: x from −2.0 up to 0.5,

00:06:33.499 --> 00:06:39.050
and y from −1.25 up to 1.25, never reaching the top edge.

00:06:39.400 --> 00:06:42.856
Those edges come only from the numbers typed in the two lines.

00:06:43.210 --> 00:06:48.412
The tempting answer, "a bigger img_size shows a bigger picture", fails: the same

00:06:48.412 --> 00:06:51.338
middleman pair just moved to a larger (i, j).

00:06:53.480 --> 00:06:55.827
A computer picture is a list of samples.

00:06:56.180 --> 00:07:01.305
The programmer decides which region of the mathematical plane to sample and how many

00:07:01.305 --> 00:07:02.281
samples to take.

00:07:02.630 --> 00:07:07.477
The arithmetic never sees the sample's number on the sheet, only the point it stands

00:07:07.477 --> 00:07:07.707
for.

00:07:08.060 --> 00:07:13.452
For a manager: when two teams report results "for the same input", ask whether they

00:07:13.452 --> 00:07:16.636
mean the same index or the same underlying value.

00:07:16.980 --> 00:07:19.796
Here the same (9, 7) meant two different points.

00:07:21.950 --> 00:07:23.123
The answer is no.

00:07:23.470 --> 00:07:39.150
At 256 the place gives (−1.375, −0.625); at 512 it gives (−1.6875, −0.9375).

00:07:39.500 --> 00:07:40.545
Mark your tally.

00:07:42.700 --> 00:07:51.060
The picture is meant to cover x from −2.0 to 0.5, yet its last place never reaches

00:07:51.060 --> 00:07:51.468
0.5.

00:07:51.820 --> 00:07:57.438
What single change to the conversion lands the last place exactly on 0.5, and what

00:07:57.438 --> 00:07:58.945
does that change cost?

00:07:59.290 --> 00:08:00.058
Pause.

00:08:06.410 --> 00:08:10.037
Divide by img_size minus 1 instead of img_size.

00:08:10.390 --> 00:08:16.598
The last j then gives 1 × 2.5 − 2.0 = 0.5.

00:08:16.950 --> 00:08:25.694
The cost is that the step becomes 2.5/255 at size 256, which is not a power-of-2

00:08:25.694 --> 00:08:26.678
fraction.

00:08:27.020 --> 00:08:32.320
The steps are then no longer exact in a float, and any shortcut that builds several

00:08:32.320 --> 00:08:36.535
cx values from a fixed step stops matching the plain line exactly.

